      <!-- Booked 2026-08-21 by task 020 (code review W-8). -->
    <criterion>/test-diet explicitly adjudicates the three auth files now living under tests/integration/seam/Auth/ (CredentialSelectionSeamTests, ConfidentialClientSharingSeamTests, ClientAssertionProviderSeamTests). ADR-038 defines seam/** as the AI convergence spine (dispatch -&gt; executor -&gt; ledger -&gt; disposition); these are credential-seam tests placed there because tests/integration/auth/** is EMPTY and NOT COMPILED into any csproj -- a test authored there would run never. Resolve deliberately: broaden the seam README with an auth/credential sub-category, or wire auth/** into the csproj and relocate. Either way do not let three files inherit deletion protection by accident.</criterion>
</acceptance-criteria>

  <booked-obligations>
    <!-- BOOKED BY TASK 031 (2026-08-24) — record: notes/decisions/031-obo-verification-dev.md §5.3 -->
    <obligation source="031" id="031-A">
      DECISION NEEDED (not a silent deferral): DELETE or RE-GUARD two superseded endpoints in
      DocumentsEndpoints.cs that return 403 to EVERY caller.

      Affected — exactly two, both lacking a resource id:
        POST /api/containers            (DocumentsEndpoints.cs:16, policy at :61)   create container
        GET  /api/containers            (DocumentsEndpoints.cs:64, policy at :106)  list containers

      Mechanism: both carry .RequireAuthorization("canmanagecontainers") ->
      ResourceAccessRequirement("create_container"), but ResourceAccessHandler is PER-RESOURCE. It
      reads route values containerId/driveId/documentId/resourceId/id, then falls back to query
      containerId/driveId/documentId/resourceId (ResourceAccessHandler.cs:139-159). A collection
      route supplies none of them — and note the near-miss: GET /api/containers takes
      containerTypeId, which the fallback does not match. So Fail() is called and the request 403s
      in ~2.7ms, before any Graph/Dataverse call. The other four endpoints on the same policy
      (/api/containers/{containerId}/drive and the three /api/drives/{driveId}/... routes) DO carry a
      resource id and work correctly.

      THE CAPABILITY IS NOT MISSING — verified 2026-08-24. Container create and list both work via
      the SpeAdmin group at /api/spe/containers (Api/SpeAdmin/ContainerEndpoints.cs:67), and
      provisioning creates containers directly via SpeFileStore.CreateContainerAsync
      (ExternalAccess/ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs:390). No client code calls /api/containers at all
      (repo-wide grep: only docs, API baselines, and one CORS OPTIONS test). These two are
      superseded duplicates, not a broken required capability.

      PRE-EXISTING, not caused by auth-v4: git diff origin/master...HEAD touches neither
      ResourceAccessHandler.cs nor DocumentsEndpoints.cs.

      Docs were the real damage and are ALREADY FIXED by task 031 (2026-08-24):
        docs/guides/INCIDENT-RESPONSE.md      told operators to health-probe /api/containers during
                                              an SPE incident — a permanent 403 that would send them
                                              chasing a non-existent auth fault
        docs/architecture/sdap-overview.md    advertised it as the container CRUD path
        docs/standards/INTEGRATION-CONTRACTS.md  listed it as the container-management contract

      What remains for the owner of the documents surface: delete the two dead endpoints, or re-guard
      them with an app-role/admin policy instead of a per-resource one. Do NOT fix this inside a
      credential-migration project.
    </obligation>

    <!-- BOOKED BY TASK 031 (2026-08-24) - record: notes/decisions/031-obo-verification-dev.md sections 5.5, 5.9, 6.2, 6.3 -->
    <obligation source="031" id="031-B">
      DOCUMENTS / SPE SURFACE - three PRE-EXISTING defects found while verifying SPE over OBO. None is
      caused by this project: every one was reproduced identically against the DEFAULT slot, which runs
      the pre-migration build on the client secret (empirical-reproduction-first, CLAUDE.md section 10 F.3).

      (1) STALE DATA. The only sprk_container row in dev holds SPE container id
          b!21yLRdWEOAdkaWXskuRfByIRiz1S9kb_xPveFbearu6y9k1_PqePezTIDObGJTYq50, which returns 404 from
          GET https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/storage/fileStorage/containers/{id}/drive. The real
          container behind the same display name ("Full Flow Test 2025-09-30 14:51:26") is
          b!rAta3Ht_zEKl6AqiQObblUhqWZU646tBrEagKKMKiOcv-7Yo7739SKCuM2H-RPAy, resolvable via
          GET /api/spe/containers?configId=c3a25b9a-e81f-f111-88b2-7c1e525abd8b.

      (2) ERROR-OPEN ENDPOINT. GET /api/obo/containers/{id}/children converts a Graph 404 into
          HTTP 200 {"items":[],"nextLink":null}. Measured live: Graph 404 at 13:59:22.915, endpoint
          returned 200 at 13:59:22.917. A missing container is therefore indistinguishable from an empty
          one. This is the same failure shape as the /api/containers runbook probe corrected under 031-A:
          an endpoint a reader would reach for as an SPE health signal returns 200 whether or not SPE
          works. It also caused a WRONG conclusion to be written into this project's own 031 record
          (retracted in section 5.5) - so it has already cost real diagnostic time once.

      (3) BROKEN QUERY. obo.children.list sends $skip to Graph. Against a REAL container it returns
          HTTP 400 invalidRequest "Query option 'Skip' is not allowed. To allow it, set the
          'AllowedQueryOptions' property on EnableQueryAttribute or QueryValidationSettings." SPE drives
          do not accept $skip; use $top + @odata.nextLink paging.

      Defect 2 masked defect 3, and defect 1 masked both. Fixing 3 without fixing 2 leaves the masking in
      place. Do NOT fix inside a credential-migration project.

      What DOES work, verified 2026-08-24 over OBO under MI-FIC against the real container:
        PUT    /api/obo/containers/{id}/files/{path}          -> 200, real driveItem
        GET    /api/obo/drives/{driveId}/items/{itemId}/content -> 200, byte-identical round-trip
        DELETE /api/obo/drives/{driveId}/items/{itemId}       -> 204
        GET    /api/spe/containers/{id}/items/{itemId}/preview?configId={guid} -> 200 (app-only SpeAdmin
               path under owning app 170c98e1-..., ADR-028 E-1 - not the BFF identity)
    </obligation>

    <obligation source="031" id="031-C">
      DECISION NEEDED: AgentTokenService is DEAD CODE - wire it up or delete it.

      It is registered as a singleton (Infrastructure/DI/AgentModule.cs:31) and was fully migrated to the
      ordered credential provider by task 022 (GetConfidentialClientAsync -> _confidentialClients
      .GetClientAsync, throwing if the provider is absent). But NO production code path reaches it. A
      repo-wide search finds its only mention inside a handler is a TODO comment at
      Api/Agent/SpaarkeAgentHandler.cs:25 ("Inject AgentTokenService when MCI-014 is implemented").
      AcquireGraphTokenAsync / AcquireDataverseTokenAsync are invoked from exactly one place in the repo:
      tests/integration/seam/Auth/ConfidentialClientMigrationSeamTests.cs:123.

      Consequence recorded honestly in 031: FR-B3 site 4 CANNOT be verified over HTTP, because no HTTP
      path reaches it. Its migration is proven at the seam-test level only. /api/agent/message returns 200
      with real data, but it does so through the chat pipeline's DataverseUserClient - that 200 is NOT
      evidence about AgentTokenService.

      Why this belongs in wrap-up rather than being ignored: a migrated confidential-client construction
      site with no caller is precisely what the Phase 6 credential census (task 061) exists to keep
      honest. Either it is a real site (wire it up, then it is verifiable end-to-end) or it is not
      (delete it, and the census count drops by one). Leaving it registered-but-unreachable makes the
      census number true but meaningless.
    </obligation>
  </booked-obligations>
</task>
